    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "The global 'hidden' option is how the user signals their expectations, so yes, dir.lua should somehow respect that. If user has set 'nohidden' globally then dir.lua should probably set `bufhidden=unload` or something like that on dir.lua buffers.\n\nAnd for more surgical configuration, we should guide users to define a `autocmd FileType directory` handler where they can set buffer-local 'bufhidden':\n\n    autocmd FileType directory setlocal bufhidden=...\n\nBut otherwise, directory buffers should behave approximately like any other \"file\" buffer. Buffers are the main UI of Vim, they exist for you to navigate-to, they show up in `:oldfiles` and `:ls`, they show up in pickers, you should be able to return-to them via global marks, ctrl-o, etc. Worrying about buffers being open is just the wrong way to use Vim :) ",
