        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": ">  Worrying about buffers being open is just the wrong way to use Vim :)\n\nI would say it's personal preference. Some users like to think in a viewport unit (tab/win) and some users like to think in a bottom-up unit (buf) (that can be used for something)\n\nBut anyway, that's not my point. Maybe I was unprecise.\n\nIf the purporse of dir.lua is to interact as a dir-navigator when \"openening\" a dir instead of a file, I think it's more wise to reuse the dir-buffer if the next chosen item is a directory too. (Reuse or close after new one)\nshort: if navigating through 10 directories, there should only be one directory buffer open, instead of 10. So there are no \"zombie\" buffers.\nThere is no need for closing the last directory buffer when a file was chosen finally. (as I wrote prior to this)\nBut I don't know the bigger picture of it - so my expectations could be incomplete.\n\nIn 99% of cases, I use a file-picker to open files, but in that other 1% there shouldn't be a lot of open buffers left opened. Maybe I'm the only one with a workflow that heavly depends on the \"open buffers\"... but less mess == less distraction",
